WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:14.470
My name Kristen, so
I'm at Irrational Labs right now.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.710
At Irrational Labs how many
of you know Dan Ariely?

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:18.660
Great.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.870
So, he's a kind of famed author,
professor at Duke.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.813
And kind of a thought leader, he is
a though leader in behavioral economics.

00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:30.692
So what Irrational Labs does is
try to merge this understanding

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:33.180
of human behavior, human psychology,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.081
and then teach products that are trying
to change the world how to better improve

00:00:38.081 --> 00:00:42.720
their users lives through understanding
the psychology of their users.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.538
Partly trying to take the academic world
and say, hey folks, there's learnings

00:00:46.538 --> 00:00:50.132
over here that you guys should be trying
to pay attention to while you go try and

00:00:50.132 --> 00:00:52.924
build products that will
change people's lives forever.

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:54.791
So, how we do that?

00:00:54.791 --> 00:01:00.645
We don't like to say consulting but
we help companies do this.

00:01:00.645 --> 00:01:06.375
We're helping Google, we've helped Intuit,
Netflix, Fidelity, Lyft.

00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:10.271
And basically they come to us with
all sorts of problems from trying to

00:01:10.271 --> 00:01:13.900
increasing willingness to pay,
to helping people save money,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.750
to helping people save time,
employing motivation, you name it.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.350
And you think about the stuff that's in
the academic world about understanding

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.600
human behavior, and it can be applied
to help them solve their problems.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.300
We also do events.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.724
So every year, we'll have start ups and
academics get in a room and hang out for

00:01:29.724 --> 00:01:32.489
about three days and
basically share insights.

00:01:32.489 --> 00:01:36.999
And they walk away with the experiment
designs to the academics learn what folks

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:41.106
in the real world are doing and
the products people actually have a theory

00:01:41.106 --> 00:01:43.960
that can shortcut a little
bit of their road maps.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.200
And then most recently,
I'm very excited about this,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.240
on Monday we're releasing workbooks.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.820
So we basically, took the last year and
a half and said, very nice, and

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.650
you can definitely read Daniel Kahneman,
Thinking Fast and Slow, Dan books.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.810
But in general, there's still
this kind of missing link between

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.840
how we talk about the learnings of
human behavior and product folks.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.360
And so these workbooks are our effort,
there's about ten of them.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.230
And they go through key behaviors and
incentives and

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.832
pricing and
social proof to try connect the gap.

00:02:11.832 --> 00:02:13.750
It is literally we want
people to print this out.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.460
I know this is crazy.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.580
I don't even know what I'm thinking.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.660
But thinking is you would sit in
a room with your product team and

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.200
think about how do you intervene
in a users life to help them

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.360
to bridge this intention and action gap.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.710
And trying to give the folks the tools
to do that versus just talking three or

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.490
five users.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.810
Which is very nice, very insightful but
we think there's probably a better way.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.980
And that's kind of what I'm going to talk
today about is there's a better way.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.410
So as a product community, you know, we
can, we're trying to change people's lives

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.260
and I think that we could be doing
it in a more successful way.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.930
The one way we could do this,
oops, I just ruined it.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.200
So we could start by looking at
the product and basically say what

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.950
are principles of product development that
would help us design better products?

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.420
But what I propose, because behavioral
economics is really about the study of how

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.330
people make decisions,
that we take more lessons from life.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.400
So, I'm going to share some lessons from
my life, and then we'll apply it and

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.140
figure out how to scale that out into
principles of product, product and

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.670
behavioral design thinking.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.810
So obviously,
every good story starts with the womb.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.510
And so I'm a twin.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.550
That’s me, this is my sister and
we're fraternal twins.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:29.000
And if you don't know about fraternal
twins what happens is that about 99.95% of

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.140
our DNA is the same just like sisters.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.627
But the thing about fraternal twins is
that we share basically everything, right?

00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:41.496
So, we shared bunk beds, obviously
parents, lunches, clothes, classes,

00:03:41.496 --> 00:03:46.017
sports, transportation to and
from school, like everything, right?

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:49.903
Actually, so the funny thing,
I told my sister this litter spiel for

00:03:49.903 --> 00:03:51.177
the slide last night.

00:03:51.177 --> 00:03:54.627
And so this is what I'm going to say and
she's like Brittany we shared everything.

00:03:54.627 --> 00:03:59.673
And then she looks at me, Kristen we
shared everything except boyfriends.

00:03:59.673 --> 00:04:03.183
Like this is a very on point comment, and
then we looked at each other and said,

00:04:03.183 --> 00:04:04.423
actually one, and I was like,

00:04:04.423 --> 00:04:07.803
wow, that's, [LAUGH],
I'll share that with the class tomorrow.

00:04:08.863 --> 00:04:12.190
And so, but if you think about this,
and how similar we are.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:16.852
This is what we look like now, this is
my sister she is a yogi, meditative,

00:04:16.852 --> 00:04:19.748
like somebody who's
like pure San Franscio.

00:04:19.748 --> 00:04:24.046
Like this girl she just represents
the culture of yogis in San Franscio and

00:04:24.046 --> 00:04:29.070
I'm this professional multitasker,
I leave during stretching exercises.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.030
Yeah, at the end of workouts,
I could be doing three things.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.020
But if I'm doing one, this is terrible.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.810
And you think about how actually
we became so different.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.870
And in reality, it's that, and
this is kind of the first lesson here.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.985
We have small details
make a big difference.

00:04:42.985 --> 00:04:45.815
All right, so the very small
details obviously in our DNA and

00:04:45.815 --> 00:04:49.160
in our environment make a huge
difference on the outcomes of our lives.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.250
We'll go on to the next one.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:55.010
So other big decisions and
maybe this has hit you guys before.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.830
Coming to Stanford,
it'll hit you after but at some point,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:01.230
you have to make a decision
about where you want to live.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:08.050
And so after school, I had a job offer
from a nice consulting company in Chicago.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.280
Or I was going to go with Intuit and
hang out in San Francisco.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.379
And so the obvious thing, you look at
how much they're going to pay you.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:20.379
And Intuit is paying me $9,000 more and
as any college kid I was like this

00:05:20.379 --> 00:05:23.650
is a very good deal,
$9,000 more in San Francisco, fabulous.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:24.541
And then you think,

00:05:24.541 --> 00:05:27.220
well this is a terrible way to
think about the world, [LAUGH].

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.554
San Francisco is actually much more
expensive as I soon found out when

00:05:31.554 --> 00:05:32.620
I got here.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.096
I looked this up and
it's 100% more for bread.

00:05:35.096 --> 00:05:38.980
[LAUGH] And it's 78% more for rent.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.520
But what I did is something
called this money illusion.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.200
So you take x and you take x plus 9,
and then you say which is better?

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.490
Now you have something that's better.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.370
It's a very easy to do equation.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.780
But the reality is that's
not what I should have done.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.910
Despite the cost of living,

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:53.928
you think about what I should
have paid attention to.

00:05:53.928 --> 00:05:57.100
And you know, the commutes.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:01.070
I'm living in San Francisco,
going to Mountain View

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:04.910
commute is not one of the things that
increases our happiness by any means.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.340
The culture, so you have all these
text folks in San Fransisco.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.190
You've got kind of like
the Midwest corn playing

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.780
dodgeball after work folks in Chicago.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.530
And then, the other thing is
obviously this concept of mating.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.712
Possibility this is what you should look

00:06:20.712 --> 00:06:22.214
at if your planning on
making a long term decision.

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:24.625
They'll have impact.

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:28.730
And there are more men in
San Fransisco less in Chicago.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:32.897
But then again, as I was doing this
research, I realized I include engineers.

00:06:32.897 --> 00:06:35.669
&gt;&gt; [SOUND]
&gt;&gt; I know.

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:36.890
[LAUGH] Just kidding guys.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.370
Totally kidding.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.695
Seeing how that would go.

00:06:41.695 --> 00:06:43.860
[LAUGH] Okay, so the point of this though.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.420
You're moving across cities,
huge life decision and you're paying

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:52.100
attention to what you're paying attention
to incorrect math in only one factor, but

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.950
it's the most salient factor.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:59.030
And so, I actually was using my
gut intuition on this, in theory,

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.810
one of the biggest decisions
that I'll make in my life, or

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.300
one thing that determines
outcome the most.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:10.741
Okay so, the next kind of life epiphany
only happened about six months ago.

00:07:10.741 --> 00:07:13.460
And so six months ago,
I started using Stitch Fix.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.110
Do we know Stitch Fix?

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.020
Trunk club for men.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.800
So these are services
that ship you clothes.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.496
You try them on, and
if you don't like them you ship them back.

00:07:24.496 --> 00:07:28.160
Kind of crazy, right?

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.212
So it's like a Zappos, but
instead of you going and ordering,

00:07:31.212 --> 00:07:34.042
they literally just ship you
a ton of clothes, six items.

00:07:34.042 --> 00:07:37.530
I don't know what Trunk Club does, ship
you items and then you ship them back.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:43.640
Before Stitch Fix, I didn't get any
compliments on my clothes, nothing.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.850
And I realized the reason I signed up for
Stitch Fix is my roommate,

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.270
I got a new room mate and
she's very, very stylish.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.116
She's like a person, for females.

00:07:51.116 --> 00:07:52.972
Like it's like, she has toe polish,

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:56.130
she has nail polish on her toes
that matches like her bag.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:58.770
This is the extent of how
stylish this woman is.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.660
I was really why don't I do this?

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.980
And it's not that I don't want to be.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.420
Stylish, that looks very nice, right?

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.390
Like who wouldn't want to look very nice,
this is great.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.250
What I realized is that it
was actually a lot of work,

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.790
being stylish is a lot of work.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.230
And so
it's not that I didn't want to be stylish,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.880
it's actually there was a ton of friction
involved with me wanting to be stylish and

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:19.870
me actually being stylish.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.500
And so Stitch Fix comes along,
ships me clothes.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:26.520
I have them, I try them on and now like
literally before six months ago I was

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:29.390
wearing black and this is the only
time that I would get compliments,

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:33.560
and now I'm actually now I'm
pointing them out so you have to,

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:37.340
hopefully it passes the bar of just
being better than just all black,

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.160
but in reality it also
makes the decision harder.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.770
And if you think about that decision and
no way could Stitch Fix could be better

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.020
at deciding what I would like than I am,
right?

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:49.150
I've answered five question questionnaire.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.826
How could I possibly like the stuff
that Stitch Fix sends me more than me

00:08:52.826 --> 00:08:56.771
infesting looking for clothes that
would actually fit and look nice on me.

00:08:56.771 --> 00:08:59.722
In reality its a lot of work to have to go

00:08:59.722 --> 00:09:04.353
shopping versus actually having
them shipped back to you.

00:09:04.353 --> 00:09:07.787
So here's kind of the assumption,
the environment around me,

00:09:07.787 --> 00:09:10.011
I moved in with my roommate, I realized,

00:09:10.011 --> 00:09:14.224
holy **** there's another world out there
of style that I could be in, right?

00:09:14.224 --> 00:09:16.039
Isn't that what we all think?

00:09:16.039 --> 00:09:20.035
I avoided friction, it take a lot of time,
and then the concept of

00:09:20.035 --> 00:09:24.771
actually just having to give stuff up
made me follow through with it, right?

00:09:24.771 --> 00:09:27.342
because I'd have to ship something
back and I had already tried it on.

00:09:30.023 --> 00:09:35.008
Okay, so what I'd like to pause it
here is basically that the fabric of

00:09:35.008 --> 00:09:39.232
human decision making is not
necessarily big principle but

00:09:39.232 --> 00:09:44.110
it's what we, the mistakes we make
in our day to day life, right?

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:47.610
So how humans behaves is not
this abstract concept but

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:52.197
it's how we make decisions and
how we fail to make good decisions, and

00:09:52.197 --> 00:09:55.570
how this could impact our future and
our outcomes.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:10:01.190
And so as someone positing this you'd say,
well this is actually true?

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.120
Where's your proof?

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.484
And so now we'll kind of go through
what the academics have said about

00:10:05.484 --> 00:10:08.612
what these life lessons are in
the fabric of our decisions, and

00:10:08.612 --> 00:10:11.283
figure out how we can apply
that to the product world.

00:10:13.661 --> 00:10:17.060
Okay, so this is,
any vegetarians in the audience?

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.920
Okay, so you won't like any of these.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.840
But when they surveyed people
about which one they like more,

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.170
it turns out without the label, equal.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.270
We start calling something 75% lean and
25% fat and all of a sudden the world

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:37.230
changes and people are very,
very negative about the 25% fat label.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.970
Now in reality nothing has changed, these
burgers are the exact same piece of meat,

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.960
and yet we kind of change our whole
frame of mind just on the small details.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.687
Of all the things that I think that
the behavioral economics world has

00:10:50.687 --> 00:10:53.806
given folks is this paying
attention to the small details and

00:10:53.806 --> 00:10:56.393
how it will change big outcomes and
big behavior.

00:10:58.729 --> 00:11:02.290
Okay, so this is can we incorrectly pay
attention to what's easily measurable?

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:03.879
Our gut intuition is not always right.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.120
Okay, so at some point in your life,
I don't know,

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.040
I gave a talk and asked people how
many people have bought houses, and

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:14.550
everyone raised their hands, I'm guessing
in this room, has anyone bought a house?

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.610
We have one house buyer,
two house, three, awesome.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:22.430
So other people can imagine a world
where you have to buy a house.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:27.440
I know its not going to happen in San
Francisco for many of us at anytime soon.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.025
So small house, big house, these are the
kind of decision you have to understand

00:11:31.025 --> 00:11:33.039
what are your preferences
in buying a house?

00:11:33.039 --> 00:11:37.302
Anyone have an idea of what
would go into making up someones

00:11:37.302 --> 00:11:40.006
preferences of purchasing a house.

00:11:40.006 --> 00:11:42.233
That is one of the biggest
decisions people will make.

00:11:42.233 --> 00:11:43.737
&gt;&gt; Family size.

00:11:43.737 --> 00:11:45.288
&gt;&gt; Family type, great.

00:11:45.288 --> 00:11:46.447
&gt;&gt; No, size.

00:11:46.447 --> 00:11:48.759
&gt;&gt; Family size, okay, yep.

00:11:48.759 --> 00:11:49.356
&gt;&gt; Location.

00:11:49.356 --> 00:11:51.037
&gt;&gt; Location, yep.

00:11:51.037 --> 00:11:52.067
&gt;&gt; Price.

00:11:52.067 --> 00:11:53.220
&gt;&gt; Price.

00:11:56.113 --> 00:11:57.040
&gt;&gt; Size?

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.326
Size, yep.

00:11:58.326 --> 00:11:59.707
&gt;&gt; Clean schools.

00:11:59.707 --> 00:12:03.843
&gt;&gt; Cleaning, schools, cleaning,
so the size basically, yeah.

00:12:03.843 --> 00:12:06.785
Preference for cleaning,
how much you like it or you don't like it.

00:12:06.785 --> 00:12:11.042
We've kind of also thought of other ones,
preference for luxury, so

00:12:11.042 --> 00:12:13.724
how much you actually
like a status symbol.

00:12:13.724 --> 00:12:17.640
Taxes could be another one depending
on what state you're from.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.510
So Uri Simonsohn did a nice study and
basically said, well,

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.120
let's actually look at
this scientifically.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.660
And he took people who were moving
from San Francisco and Alabama and

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.900
said we're going to go to Pittsburgh.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.780
And what would determine what house people

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.360
would buy if they're moving
from different cities.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:36.330
And what he did was basically take
people the same exact income, right?

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.570
So money was not a question.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.180
They had the same exact
means to purchase houses.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.080
And so
what now was the kind of of the what,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.002
how did people decide if they were
going to buy a big or small house?

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:48.500
Yep.

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:49.370
&gt;&gt; They were before.

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:52.380
&gt;&gt; Exactly, so
in a perfectly rational world,

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:57.690
the prices of the city your moving
to would be what we should look at.

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.490
Not the prices of the city
that you're moving from.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.400
Right, this is a little crazy but
if you're moving from an expensive city,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.020
you're buying an expensive house.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:08.860
Doesn't matter the prices, doesn't
matter how the house actually looks.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.240
So your preference for luxury,
or your family size or

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.730
any of these factors
that were controlled for.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:17.730
It matters actually that
where you moved from.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:18.790
So this is interesting, right,

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:22.270
because this is one of the biggest
decisions that we're going to

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:25.850
make from our wealth perspective and
giving up money in our lifetime.

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:28.800
And yet we're using it on a heuristic
that just doesn't make sense.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.476
And you could tell, because after a year
these people either downgraded if they had

00:13:32.476 --> 00:13:34.958
a big house, or
they upgraded if they had a small house.

00:13:34.958 --> 00:13:40.081
So their preference is upon moving weren't
reflective of their overall preferences

00:13:40.081 --> 00:13:44.928
and those were soon determined after about
a year living with this one decision.

00:13:47.131 --> 00:13:50.741
Okay, the last little piece of this is
it's the environment around us that

00:13:50.741 --> 00:13:51.270
matters.

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:56.140
Friction is terrible and that we
hate to give things up that we have.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:01.880
And so this is base in the 1980s
in the savings world for

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:06.500
companies, when they were trying to
offer their employees a retirement plan,

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:07.970
it was called a field of dreams approach.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:12.540
And what this meant was that they were
just going to throw out all of the savings

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.660
and options that you quoted someone.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.930
So you get to pick which
asset class you want,

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:19.020
you get to pick how much you get to save.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:24.080
All of these variables were
open to people to decide.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.590
And what happened is that researchers,
and this was a good thing, right?

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:31.560
You give people more choice, if you give
them offers, they will save, if you give

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:35.080
them options, they will save, and
that's just kind of the resounding factor.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:38.870
And what happened is that a lot of
researchers said this is very interesting,

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.670
but probably not correct.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:46.850
And so they said, if we just give people
the option to save, how many people save?

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:49.880
And then if we give people
the option to save,

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.940
but we give them the kind of
automatic enrollment check box.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:59.060
And in one case, we give them an automatic
enrollment with 3%, in one case, with 6%,

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.029
and in one case,
they don't have the automatic enrollment.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.470
Now when you have the automatic
enrollment, more people saved.

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:09.480
But the interesting thing with the study
is that when you give people automatic

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.920
enrollment on 3 or 6%,
both the same number of people saved.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.050
So again, it's not that you have
a preference, kind of like the clothing,

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:19.230
it's not that you have a preference not
to save, it's that there's too much

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.710
friction in between you doing
something and you not doing something.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.540
And so by closing the gap of friction,
you're able to meet the intention and

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:26.590
action gap.

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:31.409
That's exactly what happened here, because
if you say, I have a preference to Save or

00:15:31.409 --> 00:15:35.899
not save them these should be different,
because you actually have a preference

00:15:35.899 --> 00:15:38.756
that is 3 to 6%,
the amount that you would save.

00:15:42.306 --> 00:15:45.910
Okay, so I kind of said this as a joke.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:49.900
My lessons equal behavioral economics
principles, but in reality,

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:55.100
we're thinking about the aggregate
fabric of human decision-making and

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.210
trying to apply that to the principles
of behavioral economics.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.630
So when I say behavioral economics,
