[LAUGH] Very funny, okay.

00:16:33.356 --> 00:16:34.250
Yes.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:39.270
&gt;&gt; I guess how people either align with,
or deviate from, rational decision-making?

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.190
&gt;&gt; Yep that's beautiful.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:44.810
So the long form explanation
that I usually say,

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.960
in typical economics there's a supply and
demand curve and

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:51.880
this is very nice in theory, but
a lot of times it doesn't work.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:53.955
There are other factors acting on us,

00:16:53.955 --> 00:16:56.990
that will deviate as from
a pure supply and demand.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:01.470
So whether it be that other people
are around, or that the words on a label

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:05.110
are different, or that you think
you're going to lose something,

00:17:05.110 --> 00:17:09.280
at some point we're going to deviate from
the perfect supply and demand world.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:14.690
And behavioral economics seeks to
understand why we do that, how we do it?

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:18.470
And then the behavioral
academics seek to graph that,

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:22.360
and if you call it social science they
don't care as much about graphing, but

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:25.280
they still care about deviating
from the norm of rational behavior.

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:30.960
Cool, okay, so
we're on the same page as we go on.

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:32.480
Okay, so then the question is, okay,

