00:53:07.305 --> 00:53:10.580
And gets you attracted to going
there versus something like

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:13.990
audit system that's going to get
you fear of losing your money.

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:17.460
So in general the emotions actually
kind of drive a lot of our behavior,

00:53:17.460 --> 00:53:21.220
and it manipulating people's emotional
state at a point of decision is a big,

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:25.590
big tactic for
how we think about behavior change.

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:29.228
And it's going to be different with each
kind of product that we're talking about,

00:53:29.228 --> 00:53:31.610
so there's no like one like
you should use fear here.

00:53:31.610 --> 00:53:35.193
You know fear is very motivating,
but has long term negative effects.

00:53:35.193 --> 00:53:37.820
When you're thinking about managing your
money you don't really want to lose it.

00:53:37.820 --> 00:53:41.700
It may get you to act initially, but
what do you think about long term.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:45.703
And so there's no, unfortunately most
behavior economics is never carbon copy,

00:53:45.703 --> 00:53:47.830
here's a frame work, here's what you do.

00:53:47.830 --> 00:53:50.692
And just, it's mostly
understanding the environment, and

00:53:50.692 --> 00:53:53.340
just kind of hacking when, and
figure out how to apply it.

00:53:55.670 --> 00:53:56.880
Did I miss a question?

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:58.190
You had another one.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so,

00:53:58.190 --> 00:54:03.260
I was wondering also what's the role
of habit to a person's behavior.

00:54:03.260 --> 00:54:07.762
&gt;&gt; Yeah, habits are something interesting,
because there's kind of like habit thing

00:54:07.762 --> 00:54:12.090
where product people really want to figure
out how to make their behavior a habit.

00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:14.390
And we really want to
think deeply about that,

00:54:14.390 --> 00:54:18.610
how much do we need to be in a user's
life that we're not interfering.

00:54:18.610 --> 00:54:22.170
In general, I don't want to log in
to my accounting system everyday.

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:23.830
It just does not want to be a habit, and

00:54:23.830 --> 00:54:26.350
yet, product teams are thinking
I need to increase engagement.

00:54:26.350 --> 00:54:27.520
I need to increase engagement, and

00:54:27.520 --> 00:54:32.780
in reality how much actually has to be a
habit, and I propose not too much, right?

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:36.810
So good behaviors, even saving money,
if you default us,

00:54:36.810 --> 00:54:39.320
we don't have to think about how
much money we're saving everyday.

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:42.647
They're little decisions, but again,

00:54:42.647 --> 00:54:46.370
we really want to think carefully about
why we want habits so we'll start there.

00:54:46.370 --> 00:54:50.013
And then in general habits are something
where you want to think about,

00:54:50.013 --> 00:54:52.246
when it fits in the user's
time of the day.

00:54:52.246 --> 00:54:56.660
So we talk a lot about triggers
in the world of habits.

00:54:56.660 --> 00:55:00.390
I'm kind of high on the horse of
figuring out when you can hack my day.

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:01.840
Is it before I get on the train?

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:03.900
Is it after I get off?

00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:05.370
Is it right before I go to bed?

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:08.120
Brushing our teeth is like the biggest
universal habit that we have, and

00:55:08.120 --> 00:55:10.550
there are two set times
a day that we do it.

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:14.342
There's some reward substitution
with the toothpaste going on, but

00:55:14.342 --> 00:55:17.422
in general we have a time to do it,
and we have a place, and

00:55:17.422 --> 00:55:20.250
there's some social coordination to it.

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:23.617
So, I'm not a huge proponent of having
a habit saying we should just form habits

00:55:23.617 --> 00:55:26.900
with every product that we're doing,
because I don't think this is a good idea.

00:55:26.900 --> 00:55:29.533
But we can be very thoughtful about it,
and

00:55:29.533 --> 00:55:34.453
it's more about figuring out when during
your day we can hack your life, yeah.

00:55:34.453 --> 00:55:37.521
&gt;&gt; I'm curious about like it's
almost an arms race here.

00:55:37.521 --> 00:55:41.010
I'm going to channel [INAUDIBLE] Paris,
Google.

00:55:41.010 --> 00:55:42.398
&gt;&gt; I just talked to him right before this.

00:55:42.398 --> 00:55:44.090
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:46.275
So, you probably don't
want to talk about that.

00:55:46.275 --> 00:55:47.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So basically,

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:52.220
if you're talking about this what
I want to be, and where I am.

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:55.560
Everyone wants to be on top of the news,
on top of their email,

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:58.020
on top of the information in their lives.

00:55:58.020 --> 00:56:00.250
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; And so, what we've begun to do is,

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:04.069
is create a lot of design interventions
that allow them to achieve this

00:56:04.069 --> 00:56:08.360
immediate notifications if you get
a email, just information flying at you.

00:56:08.360 --> 00:56:09.700
You're the first person to know that.

00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:11.240
Whatever.
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:56:12.550 --> 00:56:16.427
&gt;&gt; And yet, there is a collective drain,
that's what I would argue,

00:56:16.427 --> 00:56:19.980
that when everyone's doing this,
everyone is less effective, or

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:24.800
I, despite my pension,
am less effective than I was trying to do.

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:25.300
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:56:27.578 --> 00:56:31.920
&gt;&gt; There's two ways to read this, one, do
you just read that as a collective system

00:56:31.920 --> 00:56:35.620
where we now need to re-hack across
all of these different products?

00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:40.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think it is a scary
world we're going into.

00:56:40.840 --> 00:56:44.180
Right, so everyone's kind of vying for
your attention, and I've heard someone say

00:56:44.180 --> 00:56:48.220
they've put all these Harvard grads
to make you log into Facebook more.

00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:50.028
How are we supposed to fight against this?

00:56:50.028 --> 00:56:52.670
And it's true, it's very, very difficult.

00:56:52.670 --> 00:56:54.800
So, I think there's different
approaches that you can take.

00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:56.770
One is kind of a policy, right?

00:56:56.770 --> 00:57:00.380
We're going to say in general, apps can
only have x amount of notifications,

00:57:00.380 --> 00:57:01.910
they can only trigger you so much.

00:57:01.910 --> 00:57:04.870
They can get you at these times, but
they can't get you at these times, and

00:57:04.870 --> 00:57:06.915
that's the policy way.

00:57:06.915 --> 00:57:12.114
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] I want to know
immediately when I got the email, right?

00:57:12.114 --> 00:57:16.016
&gt;&gt; Yep, so some of this is a collective
consciousness of that this is not

00:57:16.016 --> 00:57:16.770
good for us.

00:57:16.770 --> 00:57:18.850
And then the policy would
have to come from that.

00:57:18.850 --> 00:57:22.030
So I'm not a big believer in
policy being the fastest way.

00:57:22.030 --> 00:57:23.810
But you can imagine web standards,

00:57:23.810 --> 00:57:29.280
how web standards form is that there is
the uniform way of doing something became,

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:36.120
the UIs within mobile apps were
just kind of drag and drop then.

00:57:36.120 --> 00:57:39.650
And people understood the framework
of how to act and behave.

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:43.515
So one approach would be understanding,
is there always an off setting to apps?

00:57:43.515 --> 00:57:47.416
Right, where we basically add in
more features that let the user

00:57:47.416 --> 00:57:51.823
either default themselves into not
having it go off at certain times, or

00:57:51.823 --> 00:57:57.140
shutting it on silent at certain times,
or only letting certain things through.

00:57:57.140 --> 00:58:02.810
And so there's the mechanisms of defaults
that's missing from this system right now.

00:58:02.810 --> 00:58:06.067
Literally, we're just not able
to turn off or say what we want.

00:58:06.067 --> 00:58:10.234
And if we can't default
ourselves into a better state,

00:58:10.234 --> 00:58:13.090
then apps are just going to keep coming.

00:58:13.090 --> 00:58:13.850
So if we can't say no,

00:58:13.850 --> 00:58:16.910
you can't do that, then the user
has to have some control over that.

00:58:16.910 --> 00:58:22.440
And then there’s this, just in general,
kind of, how do we prioritize our time,

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:27.825
and I don't think we're there yet, where
we understand that this is a problem.

00:58:29.992 --> 00:58:34.560
So there's some social coordination there,
as well, to get this to be a problem.

00:58:34.560 --> 00:58:38.242
And so it's now looked upon that you
answer your email quickly as like a very

00:58:38.242 --> 00:58:38.890
good thing.

00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:43.151
So if you can create a social norm,
even within a small company, that this is

00:58:43.151 --> 00:58:47.230
a bad thing, you could probably help
with how people go about their day.

00:58:47.230 --> 00:58:49.527
Now we can't change the whole world, but

00:58:49.527 --> 00:58:53.726
these kind of team related social norms
are very powerful to get people not to

00:58:53.726 --> 00:58:56.890
reward behavior like responding
to an email right away.

00:58:56.890 --> 00:59:00.144
&gt;&gt; I just can't help thinking that the end
user's not only the winner here, but

00:59:00.144 --> 00:59:02.290
insist that maybe they
stop smoking as much.

00:59:02.290 --> 00:59:06.720
But also potentially a loser,
who is sort of being guided toward

00:59:06.720 --> 00:59:10.200
behaviors that they may not
have chosen to do otherwise.

00:59:10.200 --> 00:59:12.801
Imagine that you are not the cafeteria
trying to get [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:12.801 --> 00:59:17.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the good news though is that
the people who are doing the **** things

00:59:17.140 --> 00:59:18.930
have learned this long ago.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:22.715
So we're preaching really to the people
who are trying to change big behavior.

00:59:22.715 --> 00:59:27.120
because the magazine stands,
Buzzfeed, they've got it, right?

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:27.742
It's the non profits.

00:59:27.742 --> 00:59:31.555
I talked to this gal who
was running Learn Up, and

00:59:31.555 --> 00:59:35.090
she's trying to get
people to apply to jobs.

00:59:35.090 --> 00:59:39.620
So low income people, they'll apply to
jobs at Staples and Walgreens, right?

00:59:39.620 --> 00:59:44.450
And I'm like, hey Alexis, we just need
to add a deadline, so that people have

00:59:44.450 --> 00:59:47.270
a time to respond, because if you add
a deadline, we've done a test before.

00:59:47.270 --> 00:59:48.717
It increases their response rates by 10x.

00:59:48.717 --> 00:59:53.750
She's like, there is no way that I could
take that service away from people.

00:59:53.750 --> 00:59:58.508
Because we want to give the service of
having them be able to apply whenever.

00:59:58.508 --> 01:00:02.949
So the mindset really has to change for
people who are trying to do good things,

01:00:02.949 --> 01:00:07.730
because they're going uphill, right,
against human behavior, and in reality,

01:00:07.730 --> 01:00:10.220
we react very well to deadlines.

01:00:10.220 --> 01:00:12.601
Right, they're self control mechanisms for
us.

01:00:12.601 --> 01:00:16.484
But the mindset when we're trying to
do these kind of nice behavior change,

01:00:16.484 --> 01:00:20.200
you talk to a non profit, and
they're just clueless about some of this.

01:00:21.420 --> 01:00:26.020
So I agree, it's something where if you
say, in reality, we talk to Zinga folks,

01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:26.819
and they understand this.

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:31.690
We're not teaching them a ton of, although
we think we can teach them how to get more

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:34.360
enjoyment from games and
design experience better, so

01:00:34.360 --> 01:00:37.862
that people are happier if they're
wasting this amount of time, regardless.

01:00:37.862 --> 01:00:42.596
[LAUGH] No but you think about designing
an experience like this, and you think,

01:00:42.596 --> 01:00:45.223
how would you do it so
people are better off?

01:00:45.223 --> 01:00:47.990
And actually we could do it, so
people are better off, right,

01:00:47.990 --> 01:00:49.360
this is not their motivation.

01:00:51.230 --> 01:00:52.840
Yep?
&gt;&gt; Do you mind talking a little bit more

01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:58.040
about the browse bug versus the search
bug, where you can comply to social

01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:02.050
norms versus not complying to social
norms as it applies to product design?

01:01:02.050 --> 01:01:06.210
&gt;&gt; So if you think about certainty, so
if I were to hand you a coupon, and

01:01:06.210 --> 01:01:09.980
I were to either hand it to you when
you're at the check out of a store, or

01:01:09.980 --> 01:01:14.930
if I were to hand it to you right
as you were getting out of the car,

01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:16.610
which would you be more susceptible to?

01:01:18.431 --> 01:01:20.190
&gt;&gt; When I get out of the car.

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:24.330
&gt;&gt; Exactly, this is what social proof
is to browse and search is, basically,

01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:26.800
you think about someone's certainty
in what they're going to purchase,

01:01:26.800 --> 01:01:28.510
their certainty in their decisions.

01:01:28.510 --> 01:01:31.260
And social proof works best
in uncertain situations,

01:01:32.290 --> 01:01:36.275
when we don't actually know what we want,
or we don't know the right way to behave.

01:01:36.275 --> 01:01:41.730
So basically, you're browsing for
something, you don't really know.

01:01:41.730 --> 01:01:44.723
If somebody says, 80% of people
have bought this, you're like,

01:01:44.723 --> 01:01:47.424
thank you very much,
this will go into my decision framework.

01:01:47.424 --> 01:01:50.130
Versus if you say,
I actually really want this,

01:01:50.130 --> 01:01:54.060
telling me that 80% of people
already bought something else.

01:01:54.060 --> 01:01:57.560
Much harder to convince me out of it,
but it's not who I am,

01:01:57.560 --> 01:02:01.207
it's a mindset of how I'm
approaching my product decision.

01:02:01.207 --> 01:02:04.430
&gt;&gt; Have one more question?

01:02:07.020 --> 01:02:07.740
&gt;&gt; Or one more joke?

01:02:10.289 --> 01:02:14.690
&gt;&gt; A small administrative question,
actually.

01:02:14.690 --> 01:02:17.580
&gt;&gt; If this is administrative,
I'll take it after class.

01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:18.939
The website redirect is down, I know.

01:02:21.718 --> 01:02:24.160
&gt;&gt; So I guess I was going to ask
something sort of related too.

01:02:24.160 --> 01:02:27.510
Obviously, you guys are talking about
positive behavior change, and clearly,

01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:30.295
there's companies out there that are
trying induce negative behavior change or

01:02:30.295 --> 01:02:32.070
self-serving behavior change for them.

01:02:32.070 --> 01:02:33.810
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; So as individuals, though,

01:02:33.810 --> 01:02:36.240
what would you say are clues
that you can look out for

01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:40.295
as you start falling into
the funnel of being manipulated?

01:02:40.295 --> 01:02:43.511
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Interesting, so

01:02:43.511 --> 01:02:47.280
I think you want to figure out if you're
bridging the intention action gap.

01:02:48.380 --> 01:02:50.730
So does the user have
an intention to do something,

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:54.170
and are you helping them
then complete this action?

01:02:54.170 --> 01:02:57.656
So, for instance,

01:02:57.656 --> 01:03:03.406
you think about doing diabetes.

01:03:03.406 --> 01:03:07.839
Doctors are people who are saying I think
you need to exercise this much, and

01:03:07.839 --> 01:03:09.985
I think you need to eat this much, and

01:03:09.985 --> 01:03:12.770
I think you need to take
this amount of insulin.

01:03:12.770 --> 01:03:17.680
And this is very paternalistic of them
to tell us this, because in reality,

01:03:17.680 --> 01:03:21.980
what should happen is they should take me
personally as an individual and say you,

01:03:21.980 --> 01:03:26.560
personally, given all of your stats,
need to do something.

01:03:26.560 --> 01:03:28.976
Or they should say,
Kristen, just figure it out.

01:03:28.976 --> 01:03:31.623
You should be able to say
how much you have to eat and

01:03:31.623 --> 01:03:34.726
how much you have to diet and
how much you have to exercise.

01:03:34.726 --> 01:03:38.199
When in reality, these things are very,
very difficult for

01:03:38.199 --> 01:03:42.219
an individual to figure out themselves,
and it's very difficult for

01:03:42.219 --> 01:03:46.440
a doctor to just prescribe me
personally a recommendation on this.

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:49.920
And so what we end up doing is
the system is very noisy, difficult for

01:03:49.920 --> 01:03:53.210
me to figure it out, difficult to even
give me a personal recommendation.

01:03:53.210 --> 01:03:56.816
In this case, we're better just giving
a recommendation to a group of people

01:03:56.816 --> 01:03:59.150
who are in their mid 20s and
what they should do.

01:03:59.150 --> 01:04:00.990
And you think about that kind of thing.

01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:02.780
You're saying, yeah,
I'm being paternalistic and

01:04:02.780 --> 01:04:05.480
I'm telling everyone one thing
that I think they should do.

01:04:05.480 --> 01:04:07.664
But in reality,
you're making a decision on my life much,

01:04:07.664 --> 01:04:10.348
much easier because there's no way
that I could figure it out by myself.

01:04:10.348 --> 01:04:15.650
And there's no way that you personally, as
a doctor, could give me one prescription.

01:04:15.650 --> 01:04:18.190
So I think you really want to
think intention action,

01:04:18.190 --> 01:04:20.260
is it going to be harder for
the user to figure it out?

01:04:20.260 --> 01:04:23.010
because a lot of times,
we think we're data engineers,

01:04:23.010 --> 01:04:26.540
you have these data engineers at Google
saying, can't they just figure it out?

01:04:26.540 --> 01:04:29.380
Well if you can't figure it out,
how do they figure it out?

01:04:29.380 --> 01:04:32.150
And so, giving people choice
sometimes makes them worse off.

01:04:32.150 --> 01:04:38.169
Too many choices in 401(k),
people don't choose to have a retirement.

01:04:38.169 --> 01:04:41.569
This is terrible, right?

01:04:41.569 --> 01:04:43.807
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; No, it's terrible, no,

01:04:43.807 --> 01:04:45.855
we need one joke,
I can't end up, it's terrible.

01:04:45.855 --> 01:04:47.754
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right,

01:04:47.754 --> 01:04:49.034
let's thank Kristen for that.

01:04:49.034 --> 01:04:51.160
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

